erms of the intersection numbers on the moduli space of curves $$\\mathcal {M}_{g,n}$$ (the celebrated ELSV-formula). ... For the $$r$$-spin simple Hurwitz numbers ($$\\psi = y^r$$, $$y=z$$), the quasi-polynomiality was conjectured in [73] as a property of an ELSV-type formula conjectured there (see also [71]).\n#### 3.4.2 Historical remarks\n\nThe first instance of quasi-polynomiality was observed and conjectured for purely combinatorial reasons for the usual simple Hurwitz numbers in [50]. In our terms, they conjectured that for $$\\psi =y$$ and $$y=z$$, the coefficient of the expansion of $$H_{g,n}$$ is expressed as\n\n$$$$\\begin{equation} h_{g;k_1,\\dots,k_n} = A_g(k_1,\\dots,k_n) \\prod _{i=1}^n \\frac{k_i^{k_i}}{k_i!} \\end{equation}$$$$(112)\n\nfor some polynomials $$A_g\\in \\mathbb {C}[k_1,\\dots,k_n]$$. This conjecture was first proved in [41] by providing a formula for $$h_{g;k_1,\\dots,k_n}$$ in terms of the intersection numbers on the moduli space of curves $$\\mathcal {M}_{g,n}$$ (the celebrated ELSV-formula). For a long time, it was an open question whether an alternative purely combinatorial proof for quasi-polynomiality could be found, and it was settled in two different ways in [31] and [59].\n\nFor the usual orbifold Hurwitz numbers ($$\\psi =y$$, $$y=z^q$$), an ELSV-type formula that proves quasi-polynomiality is derived in [53], and two different independent combinatorial proofs are available in [35, 59]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting Nonattacking Chess Piece Placements: Bishops and Anassas (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386112221_Counting_non-attacking_chess_pieces_placements_Bishops_and_Anassas)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Ars Mathematica Contemporanea, 16(2): 549-561, 2019. ... Hanusa, and T. Zaslavsky. ... Combinatorial\n\nTypes of Nonattacking Chess Riders. ... Combin., 77(3): 326-335, 2020.\nThe Bishops’ Period.